  {
   "id": "CqxtD2WjAwAJ",
   "from": "Perry de Valpine",
   "author": "dd632bd3bbcdd7e9",
   "date": "2018-06-06T15:57:47Z",
   "subject": "Re: Problem with parameters derived from stochastic nodes: puzzling error message",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Jeremey,</div><div><br></div>You can&#39;t use conditionals like that in the model.  if-then-else is not part of the BUGS language.<div><br></div><div>The reason is that this is a declarative language.  Every declaration creates one or more nodes in a graphical model.  From that perspective, one can&#39;t have a graph with conditional relationships.  In your example it would be conceptually simple since x depends on nu in both conditional outcomes, but the difficulty would be a case where different conditional outcomes lead to different dependencies among nodes.</div><div><br></div><div>NIMBLE does support if-then-else statements that will be evaluated *once, at the time of model definition* (i.e. when calling nimbleModel).  That means that when you call nimbleModel, the conditionals are evaluated, and only one of the outcomes is built into the model.  The purpose of that is to allow alternative models to be defined from the same code.</div><div><br></div><div>You can accomplish what you want to do by writing a nimbleFunction for the way you want xi to depend on nu.  You would use:</div><div><br></div><div>xi &lt;- myFun(nu) ## in the model code</div><div><br></div><div>and, in R:</div><div><br></div><div>myFun &lt;- nimbleFunction(</div><div>run = function(nu = double(0)) {</div><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">if(nu &lt; 2){</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">    ans &lt;- log(nu) </blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  }</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  else</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">    ans &lt;- nu / 2</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"> }</blockquote><div>  return(ans)</div><div>  returnType(double(0)) </div></div><div>})<br><div><br></div><div>By doing it this way, the graph of the model can be built using the declaration that xi depends on nu, via myFun(nu).  Within myFun, the code is familiar imperative programming, so you can use conditionals as needed.</div><div><br></div><div>If there was a case where in one conditional outcome x is calculated from nu and in another outcome x is calculated from eta (say), then one would use &quot;x &lt;- myFun(nu, eta)&quot;.  From the graph perspective, x would always depend on both nu and eta, regardless of how the actual calculation (with whatever conditionals may be involved) turns out.</div><div><br></div><div>Let me know if any of that does not make sense.</div><div><br><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Wed, Jun 6, 2018 at 4:47 PM, Jeremy G Colman <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jgco...@sheffield.ac.uk</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
