   "subject": "Re: Problem with parameters derived from stochastic nodes: puzzling error message",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Only to mention that Jeremey was using the nimbleMCMC() function; not nimbleModel() as Perry addressed.<div><br></div><div>But all is ok.  Internally, nimbleMCMC() calls nimbleModel(), so everything that Pery said still holds.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Wed, Jun 6, 2018 at 11:57 AM, Perry de Valpine <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">pdeva...@berkeley.edu</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Jeremey,</div><div><br></div>You can&#39;t use conditionals like that in the model.  if-then-else is not part of the BUGS language.<div><br></div><div>The reason is that this is a declarative language.  Every declaration creates one or more nodes in a graphical model.  From that perspective, one can&#39;t have a graph with conditional relationships.  In your example it would be conceptually simple since x depends on nu in both conditional outcomes, but the difficulty would be a case where different conditional outcomes lead to different dependencies among nodes.</div><div><br></div><div>NIMBLE does support if-then-else statements that will be evaluated *once, at the time of model definition* (i.e. when calling nimbleModel).  That means that when you call nimbleModel, the conditionals are evaluated, and only one of the outcomes is built into the model.  The purpose of that is to allow alternative models to be defined from the same code.</div><div><br></div><div>You can accomplish what you want to do by writing a nimbleFunction for the way you want xi to depend on nu.  You would use:</div><div><br></div><div>xi &lt;- myFun(nu) ## in the model code</div><div><br></div><div>and, in R:</div><div><br></div><div>myFun &lt;- nimbleFunction(</div><div>run = function(nu = double(0)) {</div><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">if(nu &lt; 2){</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">    ans &lt;- log(nu) </blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  }</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  else</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">    ans &lt;- nu / 2</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"> }</blockquote><div>  return(ans)</div><div>  returnType(double(0)) </div></div><div>})<br><div><br></div><div>By doing it this way, the graph of the model can be built using the declaration that xi depends on nu, via myFun(nu).  Within myFun, the code is familiar imperative programming, so you can use conditionals as needed.</div><div><br></div><div>If there was a case where in one conditional outcome x is calculated from nu and in another outcome x is calculated from eta (say), then one would use &quot;x &lt;- myFun(nu, eta)&quot;.  From the graph perspective, x would always depend on both nu and eta, regardless of how the actual calculation (with whatever conditionals may be involved) turns out.</div><div><br></div><div>Let me know if any of that does not make sense.</div><div><div><div><br><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Wed, Jun 6, 2018 at 4:47 PM, Jeremy G Colman <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jgco...@sheffield.ac.uk</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Sorry: obvious mistake. In the second example the third line should read:<div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  nu &lt;- 2 *q<br></blockquote><div>as in the first example. </div><span><br>On Wednesday, 6 June 2018 15:41:50 UTC+1, Jeremy G Colman  wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I am trying to build a very simple MCMC model in which the 3 parameters for the density function  are simple deterministic functions of 3 stochastic nodes.  To simplify I give a simplified, ridiculous, example with just one stochastic node, and one derived parameter. If I write:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"> </blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"> Code &lt;- nimbleCode({</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  q ~ dgamma(1,  scale = 1)</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  nu &lt;- 2 *q</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  xi &lt;- log(nu) </blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">})</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"> </blockquote><div>everything is fine when I call nimbleMCMC with the appropriate data and initial values. But if, the value of xi depends conditionally on the value of nu, as in:</div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><br></blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"> Code1 &lt;- nimbleCode({</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  q ~ dgamma(1,  scale = 1)</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  nu &lt;- 2 *qtilde1</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  if(nu &lt; 2){</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">    xi &lt;- log(nu) </blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  }</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">  else</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">    xi &lt;- nu / 2</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"> })</blockquote></blockquote><div>I get the following error message when I call nimbleMCMC in exactly the same way: </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"> Error in eval(code[[2]], constantsEnv) : object &#39;nu&#39; not found  </blockquote><div><br></div><div>What is going on? What can I do? </div></div></blockquote></span></div></div><span>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;nimble-users&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">nimble-users+unsubscribe@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo post to this group, send email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">nimble...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br></span>\r\nTo view this discussion on the web visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/nimble-users/d1bac4f8-4422-4bb4-90e7-26cb1b23ec98%40googlegroups.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/nimble-users/d1bac4f8-4422-4bb4-90e7-26cb1b23ec98%2540googlegroups.com?utm_medium%3Demail%26utm_source%3Dfooter&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792478861472000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3IiYBGi4ECXO-exw2uDJBz\">https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/nimble-users/d1bac4f8-4422-4bb4-90e7-26cb1b23ec98%40googlegroups.com</a>.<div><div><br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792478861472000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3E8y0lCOf4gTE4XfweOr2_\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n</div></div></blockquote></div><br></div></div></div></div></div></div><div><div>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;nimble-users&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">nimble-users+unsubscribe@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo post to this group, send email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">nimble...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
